#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dedbafb9-efaa-590d-8c64-42ea4de83559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540194} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3118051-1cc0-525f-9156-39a096b94fde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0840977-4fb8-5c81-83a0-8548ed739ad4" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $r(u,v)=⟨u+2v,\\,u-2v,\\,v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$\n\n*Hint: $‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖=3\\sqrt{2}.$*\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(u+2v)-(u-2v)=4v.$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S&=\\iint_D f(r(u,v))‖r_u′\\times r_v′‖\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}\\iint_D 4v\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}\\int_0^{2}4v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot\\int_0^{1}1\\,\\text{d}u\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}[2v^{2}]_0^{2}[u]_0^{1}\\\\&=3\\sqrt{2}(8-0)(1-0)\\\\&=24\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb3ece9-2e49-5363-8479-d63ce467d86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-3\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540207} {:answer/id #uuid "9edb358a-6b2f-5251-a2ea-953329d65eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\, i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540242} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f2e85b-9f3f-5b77-883a-df1a0159666b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+3\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540247} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa0643c-a136-538a-a176-d4c00c10a8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\, i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540252} {:answer/id #uuid "c60121b7-6b72-5914-b1c3-354349ef083b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540247} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc745001-5085-5735-a026-d529f4e7896e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14b33a23-f56f-5c1f-9c5b-2c28f478087c" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} - x y - 3 y=5.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x y - 3 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=5.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-2,-1)=f_y(-2,-1)i-f_x(-2,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^{2} - x y - 3 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^{2} - x y - 3 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - 3)\\,i-(2 x - y)\\,j \\\\ t(-2,-1)&=(- \\left(-2\\right) - 3)\\,i-(2 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(-1)\\,i-(-3)\\,j \\\\ &=-i+3\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i+3\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f17ded5-ca36-585c-93d3-22852737e74c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540220} {:answer/id #uuid "50b54727-8222-52d6-bf39-25fb2cb1a74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540222} {:answer/id #uuid "5368847c-2c98-5c2d-b66f-7e24f9713539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, i-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540224} {:answer/id #uuid "64f57d82-9c42-56f8-9bd3-4aa8559a63be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, i-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540229} {:answer/id #uuid "7e855b98-c2ec-5b24-b37c-ab0e734973c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\left(2 + e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540222} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f19223-ad94-5155-bae5-57a2d6eee6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "373f5b6c-f455-5c38-bc63-b24912282477" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-4 - e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4 - e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)=f_y(1,e)i-f_x(1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} - x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - \\frac{3 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x - y - 3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(1,e)&=(- \\left(1\\right) - \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(1\\right) - \\left(e\\right) - 3 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- \\frac{3}{e} - 1)\\,i-(-5 - e)\\,j \\\\ &=-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-\\left(1 + \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(e + 5\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae4da1c-f1d0-5371-98bb-4287e1772bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\, i+12\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540200} {:answer/id #uuid "97ce55c9-d1a4-5c4a-a90f-9433c127d330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\, i+12\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540240} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6e5e10-ba0f-55e1-9ec1-ea274d6e0894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\, i+8\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540241} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e3d318-408f-58da-8d9b-ebfc1a41095c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\, i-4\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540249} {:answer/id #uuid "f439414e-0140-5140-aa9e-110aebc9801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\, i-8\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540241} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf010a7-6ace-5ef2-b587-b3a68b041b59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "382a9b70-c2b2-5746-b04d-f8a10b7cc350" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y=0.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)=f_y(2,1)i-f_x(2,1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} + 4 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(4 x + 4)\\,i-(- 6 x + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,1)&=(4 \\left(2\\right) + 4)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(2\\right) + 4 \\left(1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(12)\\,i-(-8)\\,j \\\\ &=12\\, i+8\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $12\\, i+8\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1fbc53-fac0-5c4b-aaf6-3946844e4a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540201} {:answer/id #uuid "163a8301-7e2c-531c-9c6f-6f48f237e3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17\\, i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540206} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0ede24-2ba8-5050-b183-fca958d7e714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-17\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540227} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8dcaf6-080c-570d-ad24-0571ef61af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+17\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540234} {:answer/id #uuid "e0456b57-1513-5532-b234-724a3d3d1a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-17\\, i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540227} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b8a546-66ee-57b2-9ed0-2a75c12297d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d7dfdec-ea45-55c1-8a36-f476d7f30b88" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + x y - y=17.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y - y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=17.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)=f_y(2,1)i-f_x(2,1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4 x^{2} + x y - y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4 x^{2} + x y - y)\\,j \\\\ &=(x - 1)\\,i-(8 x + y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,1)&=(\\left(2\\right) - 1)\\,i-(8 \\left(2\\right) + \\left(1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(1)\\,i-(17)\\,j \\\\ &=i-17\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-17\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1543914f-316c-5f25-bea9-5cdd64882bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540205} {:answer/id #uuid "51347c57-56fd-5985-8234-25791aa98247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, i+\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540223} {:answer/id #uuid "c57ea5cd-e457-5e00-bac3-dca0b7e0b6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\, i+\\left(2 - 4 e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540253} {:answer/id #uuid "d9295c28-7e01-5c51-9026-53433bd610a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540258} {:answer/id #uuid "e19aaae6-85d8-59e4-8696-f5dbf93483d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, i+\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540205} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bb9392e-0719-5c02-9f00-dcae9268c3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6983a7a6-901e-55d5-ae50-467a154dc618" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-5 + 4 e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-5 + 4 e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)=f_y(1,e)i-f_x(1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} + 4 x y - 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(4 x - \\frac{4 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x + 4 y - 4 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(1,e)&=(4 \\left(1\\right) - \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(1\\right) + 4 \\left(e\\right) - 4 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(4 - \\frac{4}{e})\\,i-(-6 + 4 e)\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(4 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(6 - 4 e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2471b1ce-3b3c-5eed-8a36-9832e1109b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540210} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4d2040-4182-5be3-a44a-f7552645059d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540226} {:answer/id #uuid "b51675f3-11de-5e01-a701-35b0d48e8352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\, i-\\left(12 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540248} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0c4a36-f063-5877-b2a8-8e62a43ef7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, i+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540260} {:answer/id #uuid "e5585efb-29db-5121-983b-79fff3bb44c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, i+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540226} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e3366b-a88a-5425-b7c8-cc96006ab234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e4f29ae-5b9a-5b4e-895c-4a8c0bd79c99" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=-14 + 2 e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-2,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-14 + 2 e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-2,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-2,e)=f_y(-2,e)i-f_x(-2,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} - x y + x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(- x + \\frac{x}{y})\\,i-(- 6 x - y + \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(-2,e)&=(- \\left(-2\\right) + \\frac{\\left(-2\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(e\\right) + \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(2 - \\frac{2}{e})\\,i-(13 - e)\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(13 - e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d8776a-52f8-5b03-afad-230e630452df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, i+\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540198} {:answer/id #uuid "13f66911-4918-5472-8ed5-ece712ae5e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, i+\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540202} {:answer/id #uuid "3f09bec7-8ba4-511e-bb9e-4fdd3cfd1b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540217} {:answer/id #uuid "a1071c6c-e329-5604-9220-72b570ae17e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\, i+\\left(4 - e^{2}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540243} {:answer/id #uuid "be99107f-4702-512a-8dad-8b6fee50ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i+\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540217} :answer-field/id #uuid "1494e5f5-3925-5664-97c6-684d5ee53a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a810e3e9-b2c4-536f-8947-8f784a5c2b2c" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=12 + 2 e^{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,e^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=12 + 2 e^{2}.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,e^{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,e^{2})=f_y(2,e^{2})i-f_x(2,e^{2})j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,e^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x^{2} + x y + 4 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(x + \\frac{4 x}{y})\\,i-(- 2 x + y + 4 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(2,e^{2})&=(\\left(2\\right) + \\frac{4 \\left(2\\right)}{\\left(e^{2}\\right)})\\,i-(- 2 \\left(2\\right) + \\left(e^{2}\\right) + 4 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2)\\,i-(4 + e^{2})\\,j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\left(\\frac{8}{e^{2}} + 2\\right)\\, i-\\left(4 + e^{2}\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46056d71-cea5-53af-8fdf-cff404969bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, i-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540218} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4e47c3-72f1-52fd-a011-ed45d16a36d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540231} {:answer/id #uuid "807a8746-590d-5ca3-b935-fec68a65d0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, i-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540236} {:answer/id #uuid "a63d9e8b-f165-56c9-8f25-849836fb7286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i-\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540244} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f9fd0b-fab0-5382-9761-af1f0838ad5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\left(8 - e\\right)\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540231} :answer-field/id #uuid "0760165d-bb2f-5a9b-a82d-7f5ce81a7b8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c639ee8d-5bf0-55f5-afcf-28f27263dda2" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=7 - e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7 - e.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,e)=f_y(-1,e)i-f_x(-1,e)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4 x^{2} + x y - 3 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(x - \\frac{3 x}{y})\\,i-(8 x + y - 3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)})\\,j \\\\ t(-1,e)&=(\\left(-1\\right) - \\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)})\\,i-(8 \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(e\\right) - 3 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)})\\,j \\\\ &=(-1 + \\frac{3}{e})\\,i-(-11 + e)\\,j \\\\ &=-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{e}\\right)\\, i+\\left(11 - e\\right)\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647f093f-c70e-501f-a19b-854f4ee2e6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\, i+3\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540228} {:answer/id #uuid "8b01a98b-7786-51e1-9821-12e5cb35fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\, i+3\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540237} {:answer/id #uuid "93657041-e7d4-5e1f-85ba-3f1961be2bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\, i-5\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540238} {:answer/id #uuid "d66fa2f1-8099-5625-9e04-b88643084408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\, i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540256} {:answer/id #uuid "faef3d57-216f-5e84-8e95-c8bff9ec8796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\, i+5\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540238} :answer-field/id #uuid "200126dc-9f4e-5085-90c2-715a2274aefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f21a8510-4223-5f98-9767-38091af42b92" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y=-6.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-6.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)=f_y(-1,-1)i-f_x(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} + x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ &=(x + 4)\\,i-(- 6 x + y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,-1)&=(\\left(-1\\right) + 4)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(3)\\,i-(5)\\,j \\\\ &=3\\, i-5\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $3\\, i-5\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283cd385-5bca-5a49-961f-5f3c308a8294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\, i-2\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540211} {:answer/id #uuid "30d43289-a901-5a14-8537-1736cf78841e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\, i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540213} {:answer/id #uuid "509a26a4-80ac-5017-946b-e0d54892286c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\, i+5\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540221} {:answer/id #uuid "79aec1d0-343a-5f5a-a08a-b7c0654982f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\, i-5\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540233} {:answer/id #uuid "ada95e6e-ebd2-5c74-b530-94603bc33dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\, i-2\\, j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540221} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e0148d4-7d50-50e0-8c98-53ebfa9e69d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f648254c-2d6c-5863-9355-58f72f9b09fb" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- 3 x^{2} - x y - y=-1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - x y - y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,-1)=f_y(1,-1)i-f_x(1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 3 x^{2} - x y - y)\\,i-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- 3 x^{2} - x y - y)\\,j \\\\ &=(- x - 1)\\,i-(- 6 x - y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,-1)&=(- \\left(1\\right) - 1)\\,i-(- 6 \\left(1\\right) - \\left(-1\\right))\\,j \\\\ &=(-2)\\,i-(-5)\\,j \\\\ &=-2\\, i+5\\, j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-2\\, i+5\\, j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d90ae4b-bf36-5b82-b2be-e660abeed08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540269} {:answer/id #uuid "30be5477-8759-5e18-b8b5-083d35cf6932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540270} {:answer/id #uuid "a2569f81-23d6-57a6-8c40-9db98a155401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540273} {:answer/id #uuid "cde1c81e-9b0c-578e-af5e-70e0a37f16d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540274} {:answer/id #uuid "d565317f-c0b5-503b-a0e8-2dad616d1004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540270} :answer-field/id #uuid "807c5f44-a0c5-5774-a41c-61960d9dc9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35cb0fe5-8d22-5821-8dbb-8da5656983ba" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e46488d991bf461eacc51ecfcd490497bf13fcdf2b7ff998b54fdc501caf40ab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $10$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2964076c4126d6277625260c45479d1a4fb2028f7e75223a0f5942b15f5a2d2.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1870c2f0-14d0-5419-98c4-385bdaed056b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a1a54eb-7389-5d8c-b81c-2180aaa70f10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55da411a-06c2-53fe-90b6-7e7539b042c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540670} :answer-field/id #uuid "af419c4b-de5d-5ced-bcb9-7a2dd2a682bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01d2d95e-a1f1-5169-b942-8df45709b352" :question/math-academy-id "q-326264" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c5e30b2851a8268c5193c2f87e3c6ab77645db4d165d83150fb504ba06ef5c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $H(6,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa5b866-e128-5753-a445-f1b463ce86be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540742} :answer-field/id #uuid "b240cd70-b283-509c-882a-a63367769e6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0d95a82d-3596-5013-a69c-59119b644955" :question/math-academy-id "q-327800" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2889b6-5645-5d8e-be53-45d82541f5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540548} {:answer/id #uuid "0f782b49-6ee2-54b8-a0c6-e6dbea2f2953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540555} {:answer/id #uuid "23c366f0-0d8c-5687-b051-7995725ed6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540592} {:answer/id #uuid "489612fd-3d5d-549b-bf4c-cf9add26f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540657} {:answer/id #uuid "b150be25-e003-5b10-9396-dabb3aa842c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=49\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540657} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1d937e-944d-5b73-91e1-cf60f6eae5c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11028c29-bebb-5855-afbb-0a0cead7b8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79288" :question/problem "![](images/13/13036c467cbe4fdd245b12c481de08ac1eb14bbfcc50653309a913ab1e9d8463.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e167622-6728-5bab-98df-dcc27cb319d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cos\\theta-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540792} :answer-field/id #uuid "917db9fa-14aa-58e3-8aee-dd6069fe2889" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a663f3-d97a-5352-8f66-80fa9894fe7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540825} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fce734-0f4e-5bea-8f2f-5a31ade44110" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5bd0d286-b955-5de9-a7f0-4b2c20b02f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327167" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41c728f9a82b563203fa0eb6944617948cf12c048be2553b803b06820d0b896.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,4)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,4).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,4),$ $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "748ac3a5-85cc-5953-936a-bedc1c90f253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540729} {:answer/id #uuid "7a45c712-d256-5991-8b85-1e21c37c1b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540733} {:answer/id #uuid "98499a59-a54e-55ee-813f-b3c78cd0eb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540782} {:answer/id #uuid "9d04eaff-79c1-5cde-9560-3f197faa247f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540790} {:answer/id #uuid "a834dcff-125a-5c3d-ad79-8fa365d3091d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e283730-f85b-5a90-97ac-ce8837023433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bd7c7cd-dbab-5060-9d8d-5c720fc231fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79277" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc9e723494b5d592b97ff1b4795eb1bc0ca7be0c4608591792eb2ac5515a9811.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7e03f86-abab-5944-93ad-4c56076946d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{50}+\\frac{y^2}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540920} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c37ef4d-2cd6-55e8-930f-488347a97b91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0a23b4a-b8a9-57f6-be01-ea66f5dec94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327807" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69716a57-1ad8-5016-aba3-02a3013b5d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540708} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1988a8-3770-5656-84e1-09ce21c8ebb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c96a497-2573-5cae-bc10-aef22b9df685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540788} :answer-field/id #uuid "da64f4a6-5c11-512f-aa4b-74545a4bd468" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb1128f6-3c64-5af7-8173-a16416a853a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-326265" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbfc3c75c4dfeb96b6c17e8b3991238bfedde590297bbb551a0386b0a9508d00.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $10$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $I(10,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=10$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18760c58-d102-5381-b95a-b10d5aa3c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540566} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce50a8b-77d6-59ce-836d-13b9fd87d4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540605} {:answer/id #uuid "2d10f3a1-f60b-5092-8a98-b5178f377426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540606} {:answer/id #uuid "ec610906-fbc8-5480-857c-9a008e48dd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540925} {:answer/id #uuid "fc203c27-163d-5635-b0ab-260fa753b78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540566} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af6fa2a-61ff-5d6c-81a0-9fd99dddbae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9617ffa-95a5-5cf0-b858-c34d537f0472" :question/math-academy-id "q-6890" :question/problem "![](images/ba/baa8ce8fbfb4ed3acb3a9de52901b80e6acf81613e0c98ed04082fec3f653c0f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e4c87f3-4ce8-5f54-8e66-3cf5f2129b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540740} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e219d9-dd31-5e5b-8130-18f358b3c57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540865} {:answer/id #uuid "cd79da99-fcd3-5dca-bdef-4e1989a7fce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540872} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1911ec-baab-54e2-a03f-5da5527b6741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540875} {:answer/id #uuid "d9432e00-21b1-55f3-84fd-c22176423a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6645ae1-82f3-5e16-a4df-5c345aba377b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db10a605-9f1c-5d1d-923c-41dc3d49c705" :question/math-academy-id "q-79283" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea74c15f3d02478a7486f967b7d2eebc780d1b5bd53ac21a81934b6438531f6d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23009b11-9874-5e52-a6ca-94d3d3dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540588} {:answer/id #uuid "23f67f20-4371-571f-9fd6-447dacee9a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540594} {:answer/id #uuid "5e84fb78-cea0-5a36-8408-61ccd964b66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540688} {:answer/id #uuid "890c4312-8661-5c0b-8c41-8603b03d5f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540759} {:answer/id #uuid "c222c4b4-2ea3-582b-a422-05b9d5f10949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f70034b-fd36-5c12-9141-c32f3cf668a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de318b1b-b80f-58da-a7ea-29f3d4789691" :question/math-academy-id "q-106396" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bccea09ccf18444f5b9505a8a753a9065ea97a5dc622208c281926c17cd5de1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0268f3-fae3-58df-a8bd-8076695ac030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540572} {:answer/id #uuid "886ec113-7b60-5e77-8efe-36c5c735707f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540754} {:answer/id #uuid "ad707447-089b-5bd7-be1f-edc935e252bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540819} {:answer/id #uuid "d14209db-88dd-5983-9126-30629f8a80b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540880} {:answer/id #uuid "daa6d892-2a21-500d-8443-31bed71ef335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540897} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b514b94-9f5f-5d34-a8ae-19775f141b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df871080-1b98-5262-9947-842390c2ab3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79274" :question/problem "![](images/00/008a79d8328b5b58cdc68a39a921125a1efa7e0b46a663b1ede9dc6773fb48e8.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/82/82436a7f248ed63683a3164d8c372433abb6f919567defa05700ebf88c50308f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4db871-9264-5abb-8b35-f8cefa4d7edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540554} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca34a0e-8d0c-5de6-a6da-86fe375f2f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540603} {:answer/id #uuid "383804ba-6e24-59d1-8b86-33a991e9821e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540617} {:answer/id #uuid "389fd57e-2d25-5ed5-a585-2bc467281b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540619} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd2ee4e-8bd6-545a-9a0a-ae52bffe93ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540603} :answer-field/id #uuid "4318b039-4a2e-5f72-894b-9e7f5517fbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7435811-f6b1-5cc6-8105-467790c4f32b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15801" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f7a51d-051b-5f61-a319-e4c035a51a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540537} {:answer/id #uuid "279c72e9-1a9f-5c00-887d-cffe33962772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540596} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeea71b-7af8-5a52-acd0-a5f37cb0cba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540680} {:answer/id #uuid "baf87542-5fd0-51dc-a1ee-ff620017e69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540841} {:answer/id #uuid "c6715b4c-89dd-5083-9a48-108fad9694a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540537} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75e67ad-7886-5cac-bd54-994109d0b711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6c896d6-3993-5114-bd3a-88e176c55230" :question/math-academy-id "q-106401" :question/problem "![](images/41/410d6ec438e066487cd6de9d4bcbf429362a5a86d5893e7820a832d2fffb9bbd.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/06/06e4a1a4d50943deff0b50eb510ca7ad4676ad6015a9503f7ce279f249078858.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a249df-c9f4-5dec-b4de-f9ba0746d97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541280} {:answer/id #uuid "26ae7e04-d8af-5bb6-8f2d-43c43c7f28f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541288} {:answer/id #uuid "5d351302-9c8c-543f-9145-afcb9c6f9577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541307} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2bc279-f829-52c9-8684-578e789779f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541309} {:answer/id #uuid "f737d865-02df-5cc1-a11b-d003929e5c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d0db58-1db4-5743-8659-a051cf062d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "173b6f27-e7f0-5312-9952-6882bf4ed1e5" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(2 x + y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(2 x + y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(u v\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(2 u\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 u\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[u^{2}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e7eb24-fc02-5f52-9c20-aae50650f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541287} {:answer/id #uuid "29af25a0-b38c-56b0-be84-926f09256318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541291} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7c1988-b4dc-5569-aa90-8360768fae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541296} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1d065e-c0c0-5bd0-86fb-b8aca3ae9250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541312} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1c4b97-5aa6-5e63-baf9-19b1c025a22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541287} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ec8df9-6aac-54db-9e1e-7c3583337cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "288adffb-1495-599b-84c2-f5316cf5e72b" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le - 3 x - y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(3\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{6}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{12}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558c003d-194c-50a3-afd9-3b3c49be6773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541299} {:answer/id #uuid "5793a4c8-0967-5ecf-bc06-c983693ff7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541302} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0da766-41f2-5b2c-8d6f-6a7f1874052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541303} {:answer/id #uuid "7a414439-4411-5e19-86bb-89d25e2b50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541314} {:answer/id #uuid "8c441f4c-3be9-5350-921e-3e75864520c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2d6119-6062-59dd-87d0-10fbb48f1490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d58106b-6575-5f8d-b378-a90d367a817e" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le - 3 x - y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{6}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18765fe5-128b-5142-9236-a3a46d45a15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541282} {:answer/id #uuid "3b47c2ca-f0bd-56f2-bf5e-2bcf0cddf7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "312" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541292} {:answer/id #uuid "5de3fc41-5f2d-5a8e-aac6-a9fab9a43655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541308} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ab7925-7495-51e7-9ff1-6521bd68a453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541337} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ad2699-dd25-5fb2-84bd-466354b5296f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541343} :answer-field/id #uuid "5606f3d8-e847-5048-bb67-787d18f89afc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5aa7bd83-f04d-5994-9b11-f8781cd4d34f" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):1\\le 3 x + 2 y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le y + 2 z\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 2\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u^{2} v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v w}{6}\\right]_{w=2}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(2\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(u^{2} v\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2 u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{4 u^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(u^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{3}}{3}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04657d2c-3ddb-58d1-9d67-dd10a54dc0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{405}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541275} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab741f5-f47f-55dd-a3f0-ae63f69ddbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541315} {:answer/id #uuid "901cc91b-26fc-5a1b-8456-c52a31fb8741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541319} {:answer/id #uuid "a1296fd9-7fec-5bcf-b59f-60a598631cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541327} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8421fb-677c-5f7e-b1a8-c484662b9b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541315} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ba0a22-e819-5244-a80b-71a49791ee76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9650f3e3-cc32-52c0-a0d3-827c40cd5abf" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 3 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le x + 2 z\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 3 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v w \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(\\frac{v \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\frac{2 v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(4\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{16 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[5 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(5\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
